  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Monday)\nIssued at 1123 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nA couple of isolated thunderstorms remain in the northwest and\nsoutheast corners of the Mid-South, otherwise much of the area\nhas dried out with a stalled frontal boundary. Lingering storms\nappear to be riding residual surface boundaries from storms\nearlier in the day, with activity expected to decrease over the\nnext few hours. Overnight into the hours near sunrise, some\npatchy fog will remain possible for areas that saw rainfall on\nMonday. Any fog that develops overnight will quickly mix out\nfollowing sunrise. As the day progresses on Tuesday, the\naforementioned boundary will continue its trek to the south.\nThere will be enough moisture present for some isolated showers\nand thunderstorms to linger through the day, but the entire area\nwill dry out by Tuesday evening. High temperatures will generally\nbe in the mid 80s across our north behind the frontal boundary,\nwith highs in the low to mid 90s across our southern tier of\ncounties that will not see relief until later in the day. If the\nforecast for Tuesday pans out, this will put Memphis with fifty\nconsecutive days of at least 90 degrees or higher. This is the\nsecond longest consecutive day streak, with a 54 day streak in\n1980 taking first place.\n\nLuckily, 1980 will likely be keeping that record as we will\nfinally break out of the 90s on Wednesday through the remainder\nof the week. With the frontal boundary clearing the area,\nnorthwesterly flow aloft will aid in bringing a more seasonable\nand dry weather pattern to the region. High temperatures will\nremain in the mid to upper 80s across the region, with a few\nlocations still approaching 90 degrees across our southern tier\nof counties. While these values are still a bit above normal for\nthe end of September, they are certainly welcome compared to the\n10 to 15 degree above normal weather we have been experiencing\nover the past month and a half.\n\nGuidance suggests that the upper-level ridge to our west will\nfinally begin to break down heading into the upcoming weekend and\nearly next week, likely signifying a pattern shift. Taking a look\nat ensemble guidance through this period, guidance does begin to\nbecome a bit more spread. ENS guidance tends to favor seasonable\ntemperatures persisting, while GEFS guidance brings a slight warm\nup back to the region. Even if the GEFS solution plays out, we\nwould not be getting close to any records again, just remaining\nslightly above seasonable normals. Unfortunately, one thing\nguidance does tend to agree on is a dry pattern persisting for\nthe foreseeable future. This will not help already worsening\ndrought conditions, that will likely continue to worsen as we\nmove through our dry season.",
